rpc: PSK-TLS encryption inside the RPC TCP transport (GGML_RPC_TLS)

All bytes of the ggml-RPC TCP transport are encrypted with mbedTLS when the
new CMake option GGML_RPC_TLS is ON (our builds always set it; default OFF
keeps upstream behavior). All new code lives in transport.cpp behind
socket_t's pimpl; ggml-rpc.cpp logic is untouched.

Protocol choice (and why):
  - TLS 1.2, ciphersuite pinned to TLS-PSK-WITH-AES-256-GCM-SHA384, mutual
    authentication via a 32-byte pre-shared key. No X.509 anywhere.
  - TLS 1.3 was considered and rejected for now: mbedTLS 3.6 binds external
    PSKs to SHA-256, which caps the AEAD at AES-128-GCM and drags in
    key-exchange-mode configuration surface. Both transport endpoints are
    always our own binaries built from the same pinned source, so protocol
    version negotiation buys nothing; one deterministic configuration is
    easier to reason about and to test. Known trade-off: plain PSK key
    exchange has no forward secrecy; the pairing flow can rotate the PSK.
  - mbedTLS 3.6 LTS, pinned to the exact release tag v3.6.7, built static
    via CMake FetchContent. Cache var IDLETOKEN_MBEDTLS_SRC points at a
    local source tree for offline builds. The stock mbedTLS config is kept:
    a hand-trimmed config (e.g. no X.509) would shave code size but adds a
    maintenance surface; X.509 is compiled yet unreachable (PSK-only
    ciphersuite, VERIFY_NONE).

Fail-closed policy (no silent fallback in either direction):
  - GGML_RPC_PSK set (64 hex chars): every connection is TLS; handshake
    failure = connection refused, one clear log line per side.
    GGML_RPC_PSK_IDENTITY optionally overrides the PSK identity hint
    (default "idletoken").
  - GGML_RPC_PSK unset: rpc-server refuses to start (exit 1) and outbound
    connects are refused, both naming GGML_RPC_PSK - unless
    GGML_RPC_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=1 is set, which prints a loud multi-line
    banner (acceptance/testing only).
  - RDMA (GGML_RPC_RDMA) moves bytes outside the TCP stream, so when a
    connection is TLS the RDMA probe is skipped entirely: no plaintext
    side channel. (Our configs do not build RDMA; this is belt and braces.)

Handshake placement:
  - client side: eager, inside socket_t::connect() - a PSK mismatch surfaces
    as a failed connect instead of an abort in the first RPC command.
  - server side: lazy, on first send/recv of the accepted socket - a failed
    handshake looks like a client that hung up, so the accept loop keeps
    serving (it treats a null accept() as fatal).

Performance: TLS fragments payloads into <=16 KiB records, so a naive BIO
multiplies syscalls/wakeups per message. The BIO therefore corks encrypted
records during tls_send (flushed in up-to-1-MiB writes) and reads ahead in
256 KiB gulps on receive. Measured on an M4 (loopback, Qwen3.5-0.8B, default
device placement): TLS decode throughput is within noise of plaintext. The
all-layers-remote worst case pays the AES-GCM cost of the ~600 KB/token
full-vocab logits fetch (~80% of plaintext on loopback); real deployments
keep the embedding + first layer on the coordinator, and on LAN links the
per-token budget dwarfs this cost.

